# HYPOTHESIS PATCH B -- crude stub, NOT a correct fix
#
# Purpose: demonstrate the gate is non-vacuous via a SECOND, genuinely
# different mechanism from hypothesis.patch.  Both turn DEFINITION.verify.sh
# from RED (exit 1) to GREEN (exit 0), but through different code paths
# in the CMD-001 lint rule.
#
# --- Shape comparison ---
#
# hypothesis.patch (Shape A):
#   File changed:  examples/patterns/task-runner.dot
#   Changed line:  182 (tool_command value)
#   Mechanism:     Wraps `git log --oneline -1 | grep -q .` inside $(...),
#                  which _strip_command_substitutions() removes before
#                  analysis.  The pipe becomes invisible to the scanner.
#   Code path hit: _check_pipe_masked_exit_code()
#                    -> _strip_command_substitutions() removes the pipe
#                    -> _find_last_bare_pipe() finds no pipe in final segment
#                    -> no diagnostic
#   Operation:     ADDS a $() wrapper; replaces the pipe expression with
#                  a command substitution form.
#
# hypothesis_b.patch (Shape B):
#   File changed:  examples/patterns/task-runner.dot
#   Changed line:  182 (tool_command value)
#   Mechanism:     Prepends `set -o pipefail; ` to the tool_command string.
#                  _has_executable_pipefail() detects the standalone `set`
#                  statement at the start of the command and returns True,
#                  causing the rule to skip the node entirely via an early
#                  `continue` -- BEFORE _strip_command_substitutions() or
#                  _find_last_bare_pipe() are even called.
#   Code path hit: _check_pipe_masked_exit_code()
#                    -> _has_executable_pipefail() returns True
#                    -> `continue` (early exit, scanner never runs)
#                    -> no diagnostic
#   Operation:     ADDS a pipefail declaration that triggers the early-exit
#                  guard.  The pipe `grep -q .` is PRESERVED verbatim.
#
# Why these are genuinely different shapes:
#   1. Different suppression mechanism:
#      Shape A: makes the pipe invisible to the scanner (strip path)
#      Shape B: prevents the scanner from running at all (early-exit guard)
#   2. Different internal functions invoked:
#      Shape A: _strip_command_substitutions() is the key function
#      Shape B: _has_executable_pipefail() is the key function
#   3. Opposite operation on the pipe itself:
#      Shape A: the pipe `grep -q .` is REPLACED with `[ -n "$(cmd)" ]`
#      Shape B: the pipe `grep -q .` is KEPT; only a prefix is added
#   4. Different file positions:
#      Shape A: changes the git log segment (end of the &&-chain)
#      Shape B: adds a new statement at the very start of the command
#
# Why this is crude:
#   `set -o pipefail` is not POSIX sh.  The engine's execution environment
#   uses /bin/sh (create_subprocess_shell), and pipefail is unavailable on
#   some /bin/sh implementations (e.g. dash on Debian/Ubuntu).  This stub
#   makes the lint rule silent but does not guarantee correct runtime behavior
#   on all platforms.  A real fix would eliminate the pipe entirely via the
#   redirect idiom or explicit exit-code capture.
#
# Verified:
#   - git apply --check: clean (no fuzz, applies at base SHA 339637e)
#   - lint(parse_dot(...)) returns 0 CMD-001 diags for ship_check after patch
#   - _has_executable_pipefail() returns True for the patched tool_command
#   - tests/test_examples_lint_clean.py + tests/test_command_content_lint.py:
#     91 passed (same count as at base SHA)
#   - DEFINITION.verify.sh: exits 0 (GREEN) with patch applied
--- a/examples/patterns/task-runner.dot
+++ b/examples/patterns/task-runner.dot
@@ -179,7 +179,7 @@
